# Shared build context for the two images built from the repo root:
#
#   docker/Dockerfile        the API — needs pyproject.toml, README.md,
#                            alembic.ini, src/, configs/, migrations/,
#                            docker/entrypoint.sh, integrations/llmlens/sdk
#   docker/Dockerfile.proxy  the proxy — needs frontend/ and admin/, which have
#                            no common parent below the repo root
#
# Everything excluded below is huge (.venv, .git, node_modules), host-specific,
# or secret. The API image never COPYs frontend/ or admin/, so carrying their
# sources in the context costs a few hundred KB of transfer and nothing in the
# resulting image.
.git
.venv
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
data
docs
tests
scripts
# ...except the preflight check, which is an operational tool: it has to run
# against the deployed config, inside the container, on the real network.
# Running it from a laptop would test the laptop's view of the world.
!scripts/preflight.py
# The web apps' *sources* are needed by Dockerfile.proxy; their installed trees
# and build output are not — `npm ci` recreates node_modules inside the build,
# and a host-built dist would be copied over anyway.
frontend/node_modules
frontend/dist
admin/node_modules
admin/dist
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
# The integrations have their own build contexts (compose points straight at
# their directories, where this file does not apply). From the root context the
# API image needs only the llmlens SDK — plus the llmlens README, which the
# SDK's pyproject references as its readme (`../README.md`).
integrations/guardrails
integrations/llmlens/*
!integrations/llmlens/sdk
!integrations/llmlens/README.md
# Cache excludes LAST: the latest matching pattern wins, so placed before the
# `!` re-inclusions above these would lose — and the sdk's __pycache__ (host
# CPython 3.14 .pyc files, useless to the 3.11 image) would ship anyway.
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.ruff_cache
